WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.307
- I'm not sick when I run.

00:00:04.307 --> 00:00:07.100
Like, I'm really, I'm just so normal.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.557
You wouldn't think someone
who goes unconscious like

00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:12.007
14 times a day runs.

00:00:12.007 --> 00:00:15.340
Like, you just wouldn't put it that way.

00:00:19.831 --> 00:00:22.685
My name is Katie Cooke, I'm 20 years old;

00:00:22.685 --> 00:00:26.518
I live in Dublin, Ireland,
and I love running.

00:00:28.298 --> 00:00:31.605
I was diagnosed with two
types of epilepsy when I

00:00:31.605 --> 00:00:33.181
was nine years old.

00:00:33.181 --> 00:00:36.725
One of which, which is
frontal lobe epilepsy.

00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:40.999
So at the moment I have 14
seizures in a 24 hour period.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:43.776
They used to be 16, 17 so
we're coming down slightly,

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:44.859
which is woo.

00:00:54.138 --> 00:00:55.209
- [Narrator] You OK?

00:00:55.209 --> 00:00:56.681
- I'm fine.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.010
(laughing)

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.275
I'm fine.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:03.527
I was getting really bad
dizziness from my medicine.

00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:07.072
So, we went into the
hospital and they loaded me,

00:01:07.072 --> 00:01:09.797
which is a term for epileptics
when you're going on like

00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:13.964
a new medicine, and I didn't
wake up until six days later.

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:19.017
I was immobile for about (mumbles) of two years.

00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:21.561
I moved from being in a
wheelchair to being in a

00:01:21.561 --> 00:01:23.353
Zimmer frame.

00:01:23.353 --> 00:01:26.652
When I gradually started
getting back into running,

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:29.695
it kind of gave me this newfound
energy, I don't know why.

00:01:29.695 --> 00:01:33.528
I put on my running boots
and started running.

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:37.447
I feel like, it's so
cliche, but you kind of feel like

00:01:37.447 --> 00:01:40.441
on top of the world, that
you could do anything.

00:01:40.441 --> 00:01:43.554
Like, I don't feel sick anymore,
until I have a seizure,

00:01:43.554 --> 00:01:44.574
obviously.

00:01:44.574 --> 00:01:46.157
Then I'm back down.

00:01:48.935 --> 00:01:50.767
- [Narrator] You alright?

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.805
- It's an amazing feeling,
like all the endorphins

00:01:52.805 --> 00:01:55.607
like pulsing through your body, it's great.

00:01:55.607 --> 00:01:58.024
It's honestly, like, amazing.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:03.047
I train alone for most of
my runs, like, during the week,

00:02:03.047 --> 00:02:06.590
however, I do have a pal for my races,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.590
like official races, which
is Dr. Colin Doherty.

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:14.297
- So, I have two roles.

00:02:14.297 --> 00:02:16.307
So first of all I'm her
doctor, the other role I play,

00:02:16.307 --> 00:02:19.707
which is to be her partner in running.

00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:21.841
My job is to make sure that
when Katie has a seizure,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.708
if she's running, that nobody
carts her off the course.

00:02:24.708 --> 00:02:26.791
Oh, Katie's having a seizure.

00:02:31.374 --> 00:02:34.199
So, before I knew Katie
she would run races,

00:02:34.199 --> 00:02:36.369
and if they were in public people would,

00:02:36.369 --> 00:02:38.404
you know, quite reasonably, insist
that she be carried off

00:02:38.404 --> 00:02:41.519
the course, much to her displeasure.

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:43.729
So, my job is to say no.

00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:47.013
Announce that I am her doctor and that
she's fine, she'll recover,

00:02:47.013 --> 00:02:49.831
and then she gets up and she runs.

00:02:49.831 --> 00:02:52.659
- I don't let anything
really get in the way of

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:54.242
living life at all.

00:02:55.343 --> 00:02:57.275
I just don't think it's the way to go.

00:02:57.275 --> 00:03:00.302
I suppose, yeah, I don't
let epilepsy get in the way

00:03:00.302 --> 00:03:01.135
at all.

